questions.question:
A test of placeOrder(cart) uses an in-memory order store, a fake mailer with an outbox, a spy for the payments adapter and a spy for the internal price cache. It ends with four assertions:

// A
assert.strictEqual(order.totalCents, 5998);
// B
assert.deepStrictEqual(mailer.outbox.map((m) => m.template), ["order-confirmation"]);
// C
assert.deepStrictEqual(payments.charge.mock.calls.map((c) => c.arguments), [[order.id, 5998]]);
// D
assert.strictEqual(priceCache.get.mock.callCount(), 2);

A teammate changes placeOrder to look up both prices with one priceCache.getMany() call. Customers see no difference. Which assertion is over-specified and will break for no good reason?
